ICU asks the likely subtags of a locale only when it uses the answer, and the calendars that ICU makes for
its own arithmetic do not ask them. Before, the first date format of a process loaded the likely-subtags
data (LikelySubtags::initLikelySubtags, about 22 million instructions without LTO) for a locale that has a
region.

- Calendar::setWeekData minimized every locale and read the result only for a locale with a region and a
  script.
- The calendar that computes the default century (IMPL_SYSTEM_DEFAULT_CENTURY, ten calendar classes) had a
  locale without a region, so its week data, which nothing reads, took the region from the likely subtags.
  en_US is what they give that locale: the same resource bundle and the same week data are read as before.
- GregorianCalendar::setGregorianChange made a calendar in the default locale to find the year and the era
  of the cutover date. It copies itself, with the default cutover.
- SimpleTimeZone::inDaylightTime made a calendar in the default locale to answer false for a zone without
  a daylight rule.

What a caller can see: the last two leave U_ZERO_ERROR where they passed on the U_USING_FALLBACK_WARNING of
that calendar (a default locale such as en_GB), and the copy does not report a time zone that it could not
allocate, as no copy of a Calendar does.

ICU's main branch has the same code at these four places. The change is not reported to ICU.

When ICU is upgraded, check that:
- uloc_addLikelySubtags("und") still gives en_Latn_US;
- GregorianCalendar has no field of its own other than the five that setGregorianChange sets on the copy
  (unicode/gregocal.h), and setLenient(true) is still the one Calendar setting that setTime depends on.

